{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"## Tenth instance, found after this epic was filed — and it sharpens the diagnosis\n\nBug a371-fb42-32c5-48bd (found by the c5b3 agent, 2026-08-05): two test modules carry a broken\n`tests.`-rooted import —\n\n    tests/unit/test_audit_ticket_page.py:21\n    tests/unit/test_audit_ticket_page_heldout.py:21\n        from tests.unit import _audit_page_helpers as H\n\n`tests` is not an importable package under CI's bare `pytest` invocation, so these imports are\nBROKEN. They do not fail today only because both files call `pytest.importorskip(\"fastapi\")` on\nthe line above, and the `[ui]` extra is absent from the lean CI suite — so collection skips\nbefore the broken import is reached.\n\n## Why this belongs in this epic rather than as an unrelated import bug\n\nIt is the family's purest form so far. The tests are not merely vacuous, they are vacuous\nCONDITIONALLY, and the condition is inverted from what anyone would assume: they read as covered\neverywhere, and they break precisely where the optional extra IS installed — i.e. exactly the\nenvironment where their coverage was supposed to matter. A skip is masking a defect rather than\nrepresenting an absent capability.\n\nThis is a distinct sub-kind from the ones in the original diagnosis, and worth adding to it:\n\n  **F. Skip-masked breakage** — a test that cannot run for an unrelated reason (missing optional\n  dependency, capability probe, platform guard) and whose skip conceals that it could not run\n  even if the dependency were present. The skip is read as \"not applicable here\", when the truth\n  is \"would fail anywhere\".\n\nNote this sub-kind is MECHANICALLY DETECTABLE and therefore lands in the recommendation's part 1\n(CI structural meta-gate), not part 2: a checker can import-resolve every skipped module and\nassert the skip is the ONLY thing preventing execution. That is a narrow, decidable question.\n\nAlso worth recording as evidence for part 3 of the recommendation (mutation proof as the\nexpectation): this was found only because an agent ran the repo-wide sweep after fixing its own\ninstance of a `tests.`-rooted import. Nothing in the lifecycle asked for that sweep. It has now\nhappened twice in one session — 34c2's sweep found 8a5e's four guards, and 7e90's found 1909's\neleven steps. Three sweeps, three multi-instance discoveries, zero of them prompted by a gate."},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1785918689420311001,"uuid":"c3316ae6-8dca-4d47-9ed5-0626704457b1"}